{
  "dom_id": "step-q351502",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014400/assets/005c8cf386537414fb0504fa26095f54fbeeaa0f88941bef41fffaf75f791ed3.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=2,$ the particle is located at the position $x=8.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=7?$\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(2)=8$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=2$ to $t=7.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=8+(-3)=5.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=5.$",
